## astrx-suite Tor configuration — the single ingress for the whole suite.
##
## Every published surface is a v3 hidden service; NO clearnet or host port is
## exposed.  The HiddenServiceDir keys live under /var/lib/tor, which the
## compose file persists in the `tor-keys` named volume, so every .onion
## address below is STABLE across restarts.
##
## Validate (once the image is built and Docker DNS is available):
##   docker compose run --rm tor --verify-config

# The container starts as root only to fix the key-volume permissions; Tor then
# drops to the unprivileged alpine tor user.  (Requires starting as root.)
User tor

# Persisted key/state directory (the tor-keys volume mounts here).
DataDirectory /var/lib/tor

# This node only originates and serves onions — never a relay or an exit.
ExitPolicy reject *:*

# --- SOCKS egress for the onion crawler ------------------------------------
# Bound to all of Tor's interfaces *inside the container*: Tor's IP on each
# Docker network is assigned dynamically, so we cannot pin the bind to the
# internal interface by address.  It is NOT published to the host.
# IsolateSOCKSAuth gives each .onion host its own circuit (the crawler sends a
# per-host SOCKS username); KeepAliveIsolateSOCKSAuth keeps it warm for reuse.
SocksPort 0.0.0.0:9050 IsolateSOCKSAuth KeepAliveIsolateSOCKSAuth
# Because the bind spans every interface, access is restricted by POLICY, not by
# address: only the `internal` network's fixed subnet (where onioncrawler-crawl
# lives) may use SOCKS.  The egress bridge — home to the opt-in clearnet crawlers
# — and the Docker host are rejected, so a compromised clearnet crawler cannot
# use Tor as an open proxy.  Keep this CIDR in sync with the `internal` network's
# ipam subnet in docker-compose.yml.
SocksPolicy accept 10.63.0.0/24
SocksPolicy reject *

# --- Published v3 hidden services ------------------------------------------
# Each maps  <onion>:80  ->  <service-container>:<port>  on the internal
# network.  The target hostnames are resolved at connection time via Docker's
# embedded DNS.

# gitweb — read-only, no-JS git web viewer
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/gitweb/
HiddenServiceVersion 3
HiddenServicePort 80 gitweb:8801

# onioncrawler — no-JS .onion search UI
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/onioncrawler_search/
HiddenServiceVersion 3
HiddenServicePort 80 onioncrawler-search:8802

# websearch — no-JS clear-web search UI
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/websearch/
HiddenServiceVersion 3
HiddenServicePort 80 websearch-serve:8803

# torrentds — no-JS DHT torrent-metadata search UI
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/torrentds_search/
HiddenServiceVersion 3
HiddenServicePort 80 torrentds-search:8804

# torrentds — HTTP BitTorrent tracker (GET /announce, /scrape).
# Its own HTTP port (8805) is mapped behind the standard onion port 80, so
# clients announce to http://<onion>/announce.  (v3 onions are TCP-only, so the
# UDP tracker is intentionally not published here.)
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/torrentds_tracker/
HiddenServiceVersion 3
HiddenServicePort 80 torrentds-tracker:8805

# suitedash — suite status dashboard
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/suitedash/
HiddenServiceVersion 3
HiddenServicePort 80 suitedash:8806
